{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-020",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "streams file system serialization sockets and HTTP clients",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "A stream is an ordered byte flow with capabilities such as reading, writing, seeking, and asynchronous I/O. The same stream abstraction can represent a file, memory buffer, network connection, or compression layer; encoding and message framing remain separate responsibilities.",
    "File APIs combine paths, permissions, handles, buffering, and operating-system behavior. Use asynchronous I/O for scalable waits, dispose owned streams and readers deterministically, and treat user-supplied paths and serialized data as untrusted input.",
    "Serialization converts values to a transport or storage representation. A serializer’s defaults, versioning policy, polymorphism rules, resource limits, and source-generation support are part of the data contract; arbitrary object deserialization can create security risks.",
    "TCP provides an ordered byte stream, not application message boundaries. UDP sends datagrams with different delivery guarantees; HTTP builds request/response semantics over transports and should normally be accessed through `HttpClient`.",
    "Reuse long-lived `HttpClient` instances with appropriate connection lifetime or use `IHttpClientFactory`. Apply cancellation, timeouts, response disposal, bounded reads, status handling, and retry rules that respect idempotency."
  ],
